{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 23, :comments ["<b>Everything that comes into fire, you shall pass through fire.</b> According to the manner of its use shall be its purging (hag'alah): if its use is with hot liquids, purge it with boiling water; and if its use is by means of fire itself, such as the spit and the grill, purge it with fire." "<b>But with the water of impurity it shall be purified.</b> It still needs to be purified of its impurity by means of the water of the ashes of the red heifer, concerning which it is said of them (Numbers 19): ״מי נדה לא זורק״ — 'the water of impurity is not sprinkled' — upon him, even though the vessel has already been purified of the absorption of forbidden substance. And from here the Rabbis, of blessed memory, derived that even to render metal vessels fit immediately from the hand of a gentile to the hand of an Israelite, they require immersion, even if they are new, and even lead-lined vessels. And the 'water of impurity' (מי נדה) written here is the water fit for immersing the menstruant therein, and they are forty se'ah. And this is why it adds here and says ״וטהר״ — 'and it shall be purified' — the verse added a second purification." "<b>And everything that does not come into fire.</b> — whose use is not by means of fire, such as cups and flasks and jugs, whose use is with cold liquids and which did not absorb forbidden substance — ״תעבירו במים״, you shall pass them through water: immerse them, and that suffices; and specifically metal vessels. And the Rabbis, of blessed memory, said: the Torah did not prohibit in a gentile's vessel prior to purging and fire-purging except a pot used the same day (קדרה בת יומא), which imparts flavor to enhance (נותן טעם לשבח), and the prohibition of absorbed substance is included in (Deuteronomy 14) ״לא תאכלו כל נבלה״ — 'you shall not eat any neveilah' — for its flavor and not its substance is forbidden by the Torah, though one is not flogged for it; but one that is not 'of the same day,' which imparts flavor to degrade (נותן טעם לפגם), is permitted by Torah law, for it is written (ibid.) ״לא תאכלו כל נבלה לגר אשר בשעריך תתננה ואכלה״ — 'you shall not eat any neveilah; you may give it to the stranger within your gates, that he may eat it' — a neveilah fit for the stranger is called neveilah, but one unfit for the stranger is not called neveilah, even regarding the substance of the prohibition; and by Rabbinic law it is forbidden to cook in it ab initio, and even after many years that the gentile cooked in it — it is a decree because of 'of the same day'; but if one transgressed and cooked, it is permitted to eat, for we hold that gentiles' vessels are not presumed to be 'of the same day,' and if so, the verse did not command to render a Midianite vessel fit from the grease of gentiles except where it is known that they are 'of the same day.'" "<b>And know</b> that the reason he mentioned here, in the war against Midian, the purging of their vessels from the prohibitions of gentiles, and did not mention the same regarding the war against Sihon and Og regarding their vessels — for they took their spoil as well, as it says (Deuteronomy 2) ״רק הבהמה בזזנו לנו ושלל הערים אשר לכדנו״ — 'only the cattle we took as prey for ourselves, and the spoil of the cities that we captured' — the reason is that there, in the war against Sihon and Og, their land was part of the inheritance of Israel, and all their spoil was permitted to them, even the forbidden items, as it says (ibid. 6) ״ובתים מלאים כל טוב״ — 'and houses full of all good' — and the Rabbis, of blessed memory, derived: קדלי דחזירי אשתרו להו — 'pots of swine were permitted to them'; but Midian — their land was not theirs, nor did they take their land; rather, to avenge their vengeance they killed them and took all their spoil, and therefore the prohibition was applied to their vessels. And likewise regarding the law of impurity that he mentioned now: ״ואתם חנו מחוץ למחנה״ — 'and you shall camp outside the camp' — for in the war against Sihon and Og, all Israel were present and impurity was permitted in the community (צבור). Also the levy (מכס) that was taken here in the war against Midian and given to the priests the Levites — as opposed to the wars against Sihon and Og — is for this same reason: because this spoil was an act of HaShem's vengeance in a land not theirs, and therefore the levy was given to the priests the Levites; but there, not only did they not give them, but they were warned not to give them anything, as it says (Numbers 18) ״וחלק לא יהיה לך בתוכם״ — 'and you shall have no portion among them' — even in spoils. And so wrote the Ramban, of blessed memory."], :book :numbers, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 31, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:37:37.293731877Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}